    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-hongzhang">Li Hongzhang</a> ignored his reform petition in 1894, Sun spent most of seventeen years in exile raising money among overseas Chinese for a series of failed uprisings against the Qing.</li><li>When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuchang">Wuchang</a> uprising of 1911 brought down the dynasty, he was elected provisional president but gave the office to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-shikai">Yuan Shikai</a> in 1912 in return for the emperor's abdication.</li><li>Fighting Yuan and the warlords from a base in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>, he allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chinese-communist-party">Chinese Communist Party</a>, and both the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the People's Republic now claim him.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Sun's best-known name in Chinese, Sun Zhongshan, comes from Nakayama, the Japanese pseudonym Tōten Miyazaki gave him while he hid in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>; his birthplace was renamed Zhongshan after his death.</p></aside>
    </section>

